Z||000000||Groot||10-27-1999||02:34 PM||||My 84 - 2M4 has a leaking manifold.<BR>Removing it is risky i think (?) because<BR>of breaking bolts etc.<BR>Welling it is difficult as it cannot<BR>be reached without removing it.<BR>Any chance that some kind of Gun Gum wil<BR>last some weeks / months ?||||reg||8
Z||000001||88 Fiero||10-27-1999||03:44 PM||||Goot...if you decided to fix that leak on your manifold for your 4 cylinder... Try and contact Hooker and see if they can still sell you the headers for the Fiero 4 Cylinder..It's worth the investment...||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||10-27-1999||07:58 PM||||The only way to do it right is to remove it to weld it. If you break a bolt then just fix that when the manifolds is off and out of the way.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-28-1999||09:18 AM||||soak the bolts with 5-56 or some other spray oil. then place a soft drift on top of them an tap them a bunch of times. then let the oil soak in awhile. that helps quite allot.<P>if they still wont come out, warm up the motor. the block heats slightly faster than the bolts.<P>I recomend replacing the manifold if you can. If yours is cracked in the same place most of them I've seen are you may have more cracks comming that haven't shown up yet.<P>Also if you do weld it, you need to hold it in position just like it was still mounted. part of the problem with these manifolds is that the "arms" aren't in line when they are made and it puts tension across the "body" of the thing. That's allot of waht causes the crackes near the arms. If you weld it, but don't hold it true, then it will simply crack again.<P>you can get this manifold off a number of FWD cars. they are all the same.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ggarsk||10-28-1999||07:10 PM||||I found a place that sells Hookers for the 4 banger. It's <A HREF="http://race-car-parts.com" TARGET=_blank>http://race-car-parts.com</A>  The cost is $275.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Terrybogin||10-28-1999||09:11 PM||||$275 is a lot for a 4 cyl header. Are there any dyno tests to show if this thing helps power. I noticed there are 2 different headers with different sized primary/collector pipe diameters. Any ideas which would be better? After all, the engine maxxes out at a low 5000 rpms so heavy breathing may not show much improvement.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Fiero||10-28-1999||10:59 PM||||Terry...I don't think that there are several headers available for the Fiero 4 cylinder.. Hooker is the only company that I know the makes this kind of headers...it's the same system that they use on the SD4 Fieros. For $275.00...that's pretty good in comparing for the V6 headers with it cost from $450.00 to $550.00 a set....||||reg||1
Z||000007||Groot||10-29-1999||07:41 AM||||Fierostore.com sells them for $ 175.00<P>I have decided to remove it, i will oil<BR>the whole thing and do it one-step-at-a-time<BR>to prevent breaking anything.<P>There are several cracks indeed, so i quess<BR>welding is not the best way but sure the<BR>cheapest.<P>What other car's manifolds would fit ?||||reg||12
Z||000008||theogre||10-29-1999||09:37 AM||||almost anything that uses the 2.5. pontiac 6000 and smaller olds cutlass modles. maybe cavalier.||||reg||1
